tions and fragmented meaning.", "MALONE:A fictional character in Samuel Beckett's novel Malone Dies; referenced as \"Malone's possessions\" illustrating Beckett's themes of memory, possession, and the self.)<|COMPLETE|>", "EDMUND HUSSERL:German philosopher and founder of phenomenology; mentioned in the text as the source of Husserlian terminology, influencing the discussion of memory, habit, retention, reproduction, and the phenomenology of time.)<|COMPLETE|>", "DESCARTES:DESCARTES refers to René Descartes, the 17th-century French philosopher and mathematician renowned for Cogito ergo sum and foundational contributions to rationalism and modern philosophy. The descriptions present Descartes as the originator of the Cartesian method, a disciplined search for clear and distinct ideas aimed at solving given problems rather than inventing them. Central to his project is Cartesian doubt, a methodological stance that tests certainty, culminating in the Cogito as a foundational act: the certainty of thinking exists even when other knowledge is undetermined, with the Cogito described as involving both determination and the undetermined existence.\n\nThe passages emphasize Descartes’ mentalist orientation, framing thought as rooted in common sense and accessible through what he regards as good sense—the claim that good sense is the most evenly distributed faculty among people. This underpins a view of thought as universal and recognizable, rather than the possession of a privileged few. In opposition to traditional definitions of human beings that rely on genus and difference (such as the label “rational animal”), Descartes offers the formulation “I think” as a singular, defining method of characterizing humanity. This substitution foregrounds the specific qualities of substance and mind, and marks a shift from outward classifications to the primacy of inner thought.\n\nThe descriptions also articulate a nuanced relation between the I and the Self. The Self is portrayed as the properly psychic organism, whose faculties participate in the comprehension of the I, highlighting Descartes’ interest in how mental life underwrites personal identity. The universal form of psychic life is expressed in the formula “I think Myself,” situating Descartes within broader debates on individuation, mind, and the nature of identity. Additionally, the text notes Descartes’ influence through references such as his Replies to Arnaud, indicating the ongoing suggestion and impact of his ideas within philosophical discourse.\n\nIn sum, DESCARTES is presented as a foundational figure whose method, emphasis on doubt and certainty, and redefinition of human nature through the primacy of thought—embodied in the Cogito and the I–Self relationship—have profoundly shaped conceptions of mind, identity, and rational inquiry in modern philosophy.", "MELANIE KLEIN:A psychoanalyst referenced in the passage, associated with concepts such as the good and bad object and the transitional object in object-relations theory", "HENRI MALDINEY:A thinker cited in the passage who argues that the infantile world is elliptical with two centres, relating to the two correlative series and the ego's topological position)<|COMPLETE|>", "JORGE LUIS BORGES:Jorge Luis Borges, Argentine writer; quoted in the text as saying the labyrinth is 'invisible, incessant'", "BORGES:Renowned Argentine writer cited in the text as the source of philosophical ideas about eternal return; referenced by name as having produced astonishing work exploring the themes of time, repetition, and multiple futures.", "TS'UI PEN:TS'UI PEN is a fictional author introduced by Jorge Luis Borges in The Garden of Forking Paths. In the story, TS'UI PEN is the creator of a sprawling manuscript whose very structure allows all possible outcomes, driving the narrative through bifurcations and the imagining of multiple futures. The text presents TS'UI PEN as the progenitor of a book whose branching, non-linear form embodies infinite possibilities rather than a single, linear sequence, thereby underpinning the tale’s exploration of time, choice, and alternative histories.\n\nIn critical reception and interpretation, TS'UI PEN is cited as the author associated with a work whose ideas center on difference, resemblance, and systems of bifurcation. The figure is portrayed as a source of foundational concepts about difference and repetition from which other ideas depart, serving as a thematic precursor or inspiration for discussions about variation, similarity, and the structure of branching sequences. Taken together, TS'UI PEN functions both as a fictional device within Borges’ narrative—the obscure progenitor whose forking manuscript enables infinite futures—and as a symbolic anchor in philosophical critique, illustrating how branching, difference, and recurrence shape narrative form and conceptual systems.", "FANG:Fictional character in Ts'ui Pen's narrative; referenced in the text as someone who may be killed or may kill, illustrating alternate outcomes and branching storylines.)<|COMPLETE|>", "EUDAXUS:Figure mentioned alongside orthodoxy in the discourse on common sense and the implicit image of thought.)<|COMPLETE|>", "RIVIERE:Riviere is a reader who discusses Artaud and is cited as someone who believes himself to be close to understanding Artaud.", "EUDOXUS:An ancient Greek figure mentioned in the text as a reference point for the concept of the common fate of species and the grounding of thought in the discussion of individuation."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-large"}}, "key": "embeddings_d39a259f18d62ca5b1539d4379078c07831a32806e8cb680ba2cb0ab4ffa564a_v2"}